{"title":"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 18","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Trigonometry)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Trigonometry)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Coordinate_Geometry)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Coordinate Geometry)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_6_(Wooga_Looga_Theorem)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6 (Wooga Looga Theorem)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In the right triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/d/6dd17eadc905d042a9f376ccc996ec45099f8fe7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ACE$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" />, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/8/bb890e788c451fed032c8b221192c5ac43790206.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC=12$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"69\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/2/a52cc54ad6f0ff8711b047fd9d15429cc7d684cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CE=16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"73\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/1/9817c48b4061635ab58933c9626ea8e73f1eeea9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EA=20$\" width=\"70\" height=\"13\" />. Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> are located on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/c/13c970cbf5e2e7029089517d5e8efbb8cade6d8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/9/2d9456e0361813641f4ce80980839e9038fe8f7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EA$\" width=\"28\" height=\"13\" />, respectively, so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/6/c56ff6dcfd62e4e93c0469964d28b362ccff9c2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB=3$\" width=\"61\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/8/aa89a8e8ba4e4df71843a8e9112cf0050ed0d3fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"65\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/4/b24262ad0499fd3956f2f3c0df10074d18c8aea0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EF=5$\" width=\"61\" height=\"12\" />. What is the ratio of the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/1/6816e3f70d91b2115986ef939cbed51ad6b813b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle DBF$\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" /> to that of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/d/6dd17eadc905d042a9f376ccc996ec45099f8fe7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ACE$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/9/8b90a1d12a3618d41a089a2890533013e611b558.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(0.5cm); defaultpen(0.8); pair C=(0,0), A=(0,12), E=(20,0); draw(A--C--E--cycle); pair B=A + 3*(C-A)/length(C-A); pair D=C + 4*(E-C)/length(E-C); pair F=E + 5*(A-E)/length(A-E); draw(B--D--F--cycle); label(&quot;$A$&quot;,A,N); label(&quot;$B$&quot;,B,W); label(&quot;$C$&quot;,C,SW); label(&quot;$D$&quot;,D,S); label(&quot;$E$&quot;,E,SE); label(&quot;$F$&quot;,F,NE); label(&quot;$3$&quot;,A--B,W); label(&quot;$9$&quot;,C--B,W); label(&quot;$4$&quot;,C--D,S); label(&quot;$12$&quot;,D--E,S); label(&quot;$5$&quot;,E--F,NE); label(&quot;$15$&quot;,F--A,NE); [/asy]\" width=\"522\" height=\"328\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/0/800a585e0ea7c939e41ae53acca735c16e6285de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathrm{(A) \\ } \\frac{1}{4} \\qquad \\mathrm{(B) \\ } \\frac{9}{25} \\qquad \\mathrm{(C) \\ } \\frac{3}{8} \\qquad \\mathrm{(D) \\ } \\frac{11}{25} \\qquad \\mathrm{(E) \\ } \\frac{7}{16}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"388\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Trigonometry.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Trigonometry)\">Solution 1 (Trigonometry)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Trigonometry)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/3/263dc6cad183b002bbc16658c481d8935e2373a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = [DBF]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"87\" height=\"18\" />. Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/d/6dd17eadc905d042a9f376ccc996ec45099f8fe7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ACE$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" /> is divided into four triangles, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/6/3265f63783f8f764b76e9f3825300b6fe5687e17.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[ACE] = [BCD] + [ABF] + [DEF] + x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"315\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>Because of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/8/4d8879abf0d7fbb06bc388aa9ab096a5c37c9bb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$SAS$\" width=\"37\" height=\"13\" /> triangle area, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/4/ae4d963db8e2c9091db4936279d163124eed4cda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac12 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 16 = \\frac12 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 4 + \\frac12 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 15 \\cdot \\sin(\\angle A) + \\frac12 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 12 \\cdot \\sin(\\angle E) + x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"523\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/3/bb3983f004e0ce9a1b3f18567676f7e97baace80.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(\\angle A) = \\frac{16}{20}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"107\" height=\"37\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/f/9bf65e089b4314c7c6197086b45b0e47f6dffe44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(\\angle E) = \\frac{12}{20}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"108\" height=\"37\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/3/ed35dfca1df357b729b5f3787ff65fa102a459ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$96 = 18 + 18 + 18 + x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"173\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/b/43b7de6d3cc40f71f68294dfd5298ddac82488e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 42$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"12\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/8/f7896c8dff2f70ba2153cd44766cc4f5507ec7c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{[DBF]}{[ACE]} = \\frac{42}{96} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}\\frac 7{16}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"190\" height=\"48\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>First of all, note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/2/cc2fe1a8781d19079f2a119c23037ae8ec2a73f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{AB}{AC} = \\frac{CD}{CE} = \\frac{EF}{EA} = \\frac 14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"183\" height=\"38\" />, and therefore\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/f/1cf56cba51d7c2a7c49ae22f15043ad2fde327a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{BC}{AC} = \\frac{DE}{CE} = \\frac{FA}{EA} = \\frac 34$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"183\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>Draw the height from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> onto <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> as in the picture below:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/a/e3a991ca861b53810c15c17e5a93234eddefa082.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(0.5cm); defaultpen(0.8); pair C=(0,0), A=(0,12), E=(20,0); draw(A--C--E--cycle); pair B=A + 3*(C-A)/length(C-A); pair D=C + 4*(E-C)/length(E-C); pair F=E + 5*(A-E)/length(A-E); draw(B--D--F--cycle); label(&quot;$A$&quot;,A,N); label(&quot;$B$&quot;,B,W); label(&quot;$C$&quot;,C,SW); label(&quot;$D$&quot;,D,S); label(&quot;$E$&quot;,E,SE); label(&quot;$F$&quot;,F,NE); label(&quot;$3$&quot;,A--B,W); label(&quot;$9$&quot;,0.5*C + 0.5*B,4*W); label(&quot;$4$&quot;,C--D,S); label(&quot;$12$&quot;,D--E,S); label(&quot;$5$&quot;,E--F,NE); label(&quot;$15$&quot;,F--A,NE);  pair G = intersectionpoint(F -- (F-(100,0)), A--C); draw(F--G, dashed); label(&quot;$G$&quot;,G,W); [/asy]\" width=\"532\" height=\"328\" />\n</p><p>Now consider the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/7/1b7ee1894b9868699832ce22d92598b8f0b8997a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABF$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" />. Clearly the triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/4/9e4cae83c0fa7a0b33f58a5ec88e27c870c017a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AFG$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/9/f391e223e8b12650009ab6580d0b260c47e39de1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AEC$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" /> are similar, as they have all angles equal. Their ratio is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/a/2da06e42a678d0cd6cc978f8e6686dff3fcf77a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {AF}{AE} = \\frac 34$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"68\" height=\"38\" />, hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/f/f7f8451967a439796558a4d8afe4c38b45f086ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$FG = \\frac 34 \\cdot CE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"107\" height=\"38\" />. \nNow the area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/3/6a3bd8aa6b2d347e292438e7971ddfdab3a496b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_{ABF}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"41\" height=\"15\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/7/1b7ee1894b9868699832ce22d92598b8f0b8997a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABF$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" /> can be computed as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/0/5707677f5a006a97607925fffacc0193c9057fd0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_{ABF} = \\frac 12 \\cdot AB \\cdot FG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"163\" height=\"37\" /> = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/a/23ac138391a8d316e10d2583783eccf72ee1d757.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac 12 \\cdot \\left( \\frac 14 \\cdot AC \\right) \\cdot \\left( \\frac 34 \\cdot EC \\right) = \\frac 14 \\cdot \\frac 34 \\cdot S_{ACE}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"319\" height=\"43\" />.\n</p><p>Similarly we can find that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/d/d9dc75b619cd09f044d2b7ad54477d039b604403.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_{BCD} = S_{DEF} = \\frac 3{16}\\cdot S_{ACE}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"213\" height=\"38\" /> as well. \n</p><p>Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/4/e54496a3d87b7a0f75d8155bcd929c4a64f0ebba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_{BDF} = S_{ACE} - 3\\cdot\\left( \\frac 3{16} \\cdot S_{ACE} \\right) = \\frac 7{16} \\cdot S_{ACE}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"360\" height=\"43\" />, and the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/4/3f4274bcc7c9acd8f9401795bcc0b4f6a005034d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{S_{BDF}}{S_{ACE}} = \\boxed{\\frac 7{16}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"106\" height=\"48\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Coordinate_Geometry.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Coordinate_Geometry)\">Solution 3 (Coordinate Geometry)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Coordinate Geometry)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We will put triangle ACE on a xy-coordinate plane with C being the origin. The area of triangle ACE is 96. To find the area of triangle DBF, let D be (4, 0), let B be (0, 9), and let F be (12, 3). You can then use the shoelace theorem to find the area of DBF, which is 42. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/e/2de14601cab1b4b6423e8dbccee2e9838808ee01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {42}{96} = \\boxed{\\frac 7{16}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"80\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>You can also place a point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/c/13c970cbf5e2e7029089517d5e8efbb8cade6d8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/2/73205379672e860eb250c541187600fae3a3e225.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CX$\" width=\"30\" height=\"12\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, creating trapezoid <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/c/87c00688bafd1043b1792ff2b3424c4bf287ff74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CBFX$\" width=\"58\" height=\"12\" />. Then, you can find the area of the trapezoid, subtract the area of the two right triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/5/4b5390a8fcad5411742138e6fb9eb08c0ae889c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DFX$\" width=\"45\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/a/d7ada763e99852a6e44a15d46cce3c3709a944d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, divide by the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" />, and get the ratio of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/b/10b445b7deae82aa193344f9b4ceb7a42bfa44ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7/16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"38\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>It is well known that for when two triangles share an angle, the two sides around the shared angle is proportional to the areas of each of the two triangles. \n</p><p>We can find all the ratios of the triangles except for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/5/2a5d814d335a5607a8394ca87aa6efb11e2d2e59.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{BDF}$\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" /> and then subtract from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/d/6cdd1b53e5faf7a118222e4b7571d1e9afa0cf56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>In this case, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/4/41434d8a7063c134458eccf34864dbebe86e5298.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{ABF}$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" /> sharing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/4/4f43bae270243d086d23357db1731f8591fcd0a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{A}$\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/5/4558dac720390e26adeddd9fade787a9d5f8c731.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{ACE}$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Therefore, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/9/f09dc868199171f08754c1a03b3b6e47d0d40407.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{[ABF]}{[ACE]}=\\frac{AB}{AC} \\cdot \\frac{AF}{AE} = \\frac{3}{12} \\cdot \\frac{15}{20} = \\frac{3}{16}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"290\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Also note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/8/2c8fbde67cd00fc3f896e4fdeebad463723bdc06.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{EFD}$\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" /> shares <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/9/429cbd9b7c6313369455e599791f17c450d60129.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{E}$\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/c/9fca401a1bddc1bb09d51e09ebd3bfe90108238e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{EAC}$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Therefore, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/2/b22cd0ff62a45aa64dc2df3c340cbb54a005c106.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{[EFD]}{[EAC]}=\\frac{ED}{EC} \\cdot \\frac{EF}{EA} = \\frac{12}{16} \\cdot \\frac{5}{20} = \\frac{3}{16}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"294\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Lastly, note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/e/e3ec9771309fd5e7cbbd9419d6dae3a699d04079.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{CDB}$\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" /> shares <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/c/c1c62e045cad256d9c4a24cb167819b32f1f3ae3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{C}$\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/0/b50be8d6b80f756bfced94b32a8f2f460136dfb2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{CAE}$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Therefore, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/6/2963d21cb876ab1db9363f1df6653bc5e1412c34.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{[CDB]}{[CAE]}=\\frac{CD}{CE} \\cdot \\frac{CB}{CA} = \\frac{9}{12} \\cdot \\frac{4}{16} = \\frac{3}{16}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"294\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Thus, the ratio of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/3/ad335c13c2872b014a54a4ee6906bb4128f36948.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{DBF}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/5/4558dac720390e26adeddd9fade787a9d5f8c731.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{ACE}$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/1/5e10775d949a5a9d5d50424dd90c1624187287a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1- \\left( \\frac{3}{16}+\\frac{3}{16}+\\frac{3}{16} \\right)=1-\\frac{9}{16}=\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)} \\frac {7}{16}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"331\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>~mathboy282\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_6_.28Wooga_Looga_Theorem.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6_(Wooga_Looga_Theorem)\">Solution 6 (Wooga Looga Theorem)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6 (Wooga Looga Theorem)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/2/972cb62c0bab933bda11e7cfae680471fab9deaf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{CB}{BA}=\\frac{AF}{FE}=\\frac{ED}{DC}=3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"181\" height=\"38\" />, so by the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=The_Devil%27s_Triangle\" title=\"The Devil&#39;s Triangle\">The Devil's Triangle</a> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/8/838454a7bc014e7965a81644f227424906cd0a40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{[DBF]}{[ACE]}=\\frac{3^2-3+1}{(3+1)^2}=\\boxed{\\frac7{16}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"224\" height=\"48\" />.\n</p><p>~jasperE3\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>STEP BY STEP SOLUTION BY SCHOLARS FOUNDATION\n<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/NlYJJ8GU7LE?si=v4C5W_y11GHdeVIt&amp;t=481\">https://youtu.be/NlYJJ8GU7LE?si=v4C5W_y11GHdeVIt&amp;t=481</a>\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B\">2004 AMC 10B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_17\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 17\">Problem 17</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_19\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 19\">Problem 19</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_7\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_8\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_10\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 13\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 14\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wi